A||N||10||kornholio||fuel pump replacement||||1||
Z||000000||kornholio||10-07-2000||02:45 PM||||Hi,<BR>How long does it take a mechanic to replace a fuel pump?  I think mine is on its way out.  It's been making a whining sound for the last couple of months and just recently the fuel guage has not been reading right.  What happens when the fuel pump goes?  Thanks.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fearless Freep||10-07-2000||03:01 PM||||When my fuel pump went out, it was immediately after getting a tank of gas!  Drove to work and everything was fine. Turned the car off and it never started after that.  Fortunately my mech. was just a bumper push (about 100 yds) from where I worked.<P>As for how long it took, they drained the tank and then had to drop the fuel tank etc.  I would estimate that he took maybe 3 hours or so.  Probably could have been alot faster had my tank not been full!||||reg||1
Z||000002||mrfiero||10-07-2000||03:02 PM||||When the fuel pump goes, you don't.  When a fuel pump dies the car usually won't even start....it'll turn over just fine, but won't fire.<P>Sometimes they will still sort of work, but the fuel pressure just gets worse and worse until it is unable to maintain an idle.<P>What you need to do is hook up a fuel pressure gauge to the schraeder valve to find out what the pump is putting out.  It should be about 45 psi.<P>As far as how long it'll take a shop.....probably about 6 hours (at least that's what you'll be billed for).  Actual "straight" time is less than 2 hours.  I know I can do one in that time or less.<P>Where are you located?  If you are close to Denver and want to drop in I'll do it for the price of parts + $100 (what a deal!).     ||||reg||1
Z||000003||kornholio||10-07-2000||03:40 PM||||I'm nowhere near Denver...I'm in Ontario.||||reg||1
Z||000004||mrfiero||10-07-2000||07:28 PM||||Oh well.<P><BR>It really isn't too bad of a job, unless it's really rusty under there.  Air tools make the job much easier too.  <P>The "Fiero Shop" is in Toronto......you may give them a call to find out what they would charge.||||reg||1
Z||000005||V8Fiero||10-07-2000||07:42 PM||||If you are any good with hand tools and have jack stands and a hydraulic jack, you can do it yourself. I hate taking my vehicle to ANY mechanic. They always seem to find everything else on the car bad or going bad. Ive had my tank out four times already. (longer story about NAPA and their parts). First time took me about 45 minutes to get it out.....<BR>Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000006||V8Fiero||10-07-2000||07:49 PM||||If you are any good with hand tools and have jack stands and a hydraulic jack, you can do it yourself. I hate taking my vehicle to ANY mechanic. They always seem to find everything else on the car bad or going bad. Ive had my tank out four times already. (longer story about NAPA and their parts). First time took me about 45 minutes to get it out.....<BR>Good Luck||||reg||1
Z||000007||862M4inCA||10-08-2000||02:57 AM||||I just recently did a fuel pump replacement on mine. Taking my sweet time I got the job done in about 3 hours. The most difficult thing I ran into while doing the job was removing the old lines from the tank since it's never been removed before. The largest one (fuel filler tube) was the hardest one to get off, and put back on. Otherwise it was a pretty straight forward job. Just supported the tank with a floor jack, removed the 4 bolts for the crossmember support under the tank. Then the two straps at the front and rear. Lowered and moved the tank forward enuf to remove all the hoses, and the thing dropped right out. You will also want to remember to feed all of the wiring for the pump/sender so you don't accidentally rip the wiring. It's also a good idea to make certain that you rewire the connections to the pump correctly. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Philphine||10-08-2000||02:54 PM||||i've done it a couple of times but had other repair done in bettween (tank repair on one and replacement on another), so it took longer for me. the job itself isn't that hard. try to get as much gas out as possible and get it jacked up as high as you can safely.||||reg||1
Z||000009||EdsB52||10-09-2000||04:06 AM||||Don't damage the filler hose or vent hose next to it because our friends at GM don't make it anymore. They cost about 10 bucks two years ago, now you can't 'em. If it's rotted, you'll have to get some fuel hose stock from NAPA. As for the small network of vent lines, I just mark 'em and cut "em. That saves a lot of time and headache. It's a filthy job........have fun  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/tongue.gif">. <P>Oh, make sure to change that 3" rubber hose inside the tank that runs from the pump to the steel line inside the tank. I had one split longitudinally. With the pump running, it bypassed 90% of the fuel and the engine wouldn't stay running.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Indiana_resto_guy||10-09-2000||08:56 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by mrfiero:<BR><B>When the fuel pump goes, you don't.  When a fuel pump dies the car usually won't even start....it'll turn over just fine, but won't fire.<P>Sometimes they will still sort of work, but the fuel pressure just gets worse and worse until it is unable to maintain an idle.<P>What you need to do is hook up a fuel pressure gauge to the schraeder valve to find out what the pump is putting out.  It should be about 45 psi.<P>As far as how long it'll take a shop.....probably about 6 hours (at least that's what you'll be billed for).  Actual "straight" time is less than 2 hours.  I know I can do one in that time or less.<P>Where are you located?  If you are close to Denver and want to drop in I'll do it for the price of parts + $100 (what a deal!).     </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Kornholio,<BR>45 p.s.i. is for a v-6 car. If yours is a 4 banger it should be between 9-13 p.s.i.<BR>||||reg||1
